$$Z=\{y, z_{k,i}: 2\leq k\leq d, 0\leq i\leq m-1\}$$ is a basis of $H$. Note that $|Z| = 1 + m(d-1) \ge 4$. Let $\alpha$ denote the automorphism of $H$ induced by the conjugation by $x_1^s$, and let $N$ denote the subgroup of $\Aut(H)$ generated by the $\SAut(H)$-conjugates of $x_1^s$.
By Lemma \ref{lem-alt-normally-generates}, it is enough to show that $N \cap \Alt(Z)$ is non-trivial. The remainder of the proof consists of constructing a non-trivial element in $N \cap \Alt(Z)$.

Since $m\nmid s$, one can write $s=mq+r$ with $ 1 \leq r \leq m-1$, so that $x_1^s = y^q x_1^r$. We have $\alpha(y)=y$, $\alpha(z_{k,i} ) = y^q \, z_{k,i+r} \, y^{-q}$ for $0\leq i \leq m-1-r$ and 
$\alpha(z_{k,i} ) =y^{q+1} \, z_{k,i+r-m} \, y^{-q-1}$ for $m-r\leq i \leq m-1$ . 
\skv

{\it Case 1:  $d \ge 3$}.  Define $\tau\in \SAut(H)$ by $\tau(z_{2,0})=z_{3,0}^{-1}$, $\tau(z_{3,0})= z_{2,0}$, and $\tau(z)=z$
