\abstract{%The nature of the CP phase in neutrino oscillations remains unresolved. Atmospheric neutrinos undergo flavour conversions that depend on this phase. Liquid scintillator detectors of a few kilotons can explore the low-energy range of the atmospheric neutrino flux. We calculate the expected number of events as a function of the neutrino energy and zenith angle for a typical liquid scintillator neutrino detector under a varying CP phase for different detector sites. We assume a $250\,\mathrm{kt}\,\mathrm{years}$ exposure and include a typical detector response and a background model. Further, we model the neutrino flavour identification performance. The CP-violation sensitivity is estimated using a Poissonian likelihood analysis. In the case of maximal CP-violation, the sensitivity can reach $4 \, \sigma$ for perfect flavour identification. While a decrease in the identification decreases the sensitivity, a maximal sensitivity of $2 \, \sigma$ can be reached for $90\,\%$ flavour identification accuracy. 

The detection of CP violation in neutrino oscillations is one of the most important goals of the next generation of neutrino experiments.
Here we study the detectability of the CP-violating phase in the oscillation of atmospheric neutrinos.
Liquid scintillator detectors of a few kilotons can probe the low-energy range of the atmospheric neutrino flux. 
We calculate the expected rate, spectrum, and zenith angle distribution for a typical liquid scintillator detector for different detector sites. 
We include a typical detector response with different capabilities for flavour identification and a background model. 
The sensitivity is estimated using a Poisson likelihood analysis. 
In the case of maximal CP violation, the sensitivity can reach {$4\,\sigma$} ($90\%~c.l.$) for perfect flavour identification.}


