\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Axions, including the QCD axion initially introduced to address the strong CP problem~\cite{Preskill:1982cy,Abbott:1982af,Dine:1982ah} and axion-like particles (ALPs) predicted in theories with extra dimensions~\cite{Svrcek:2006yi,Arvanitaki:2009fg}, are well-motivated candidates beyond the Standard Model of particle physics. These ultralight bosons, with masses ranging from approximately $10^{-22}$ to $1$\,eV, can serve as dark matter (DM) candidates and can exhibit high occupation numbers and behave as coherently oscillating fields that give rise to various wave-like phenomena. These properties have spurred extensive searches through terrestrial experiments and astrophysical observations.


Searches for ultralight bosons often focus on their interaction portals with the Standard Model sectors. One of the most precise search methods involves modulating the electromagnetic fine-structure constant, $\alpha_{\rm EM}$, through couplings with the electromagnetic kinetic term $F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$~\cite{Uzan:2002vq,Olive:2007aj,Uzan:2010pm,Stadnik:2015kia,Martins:2017yxk,Safronova:2017xyt,Uzan:2024ded}. This modulation can occur directly in the case of dilatons~\cite{Taylor:1988nw,Damour:1994ya,Antoniadis:1998ig,Damour:2010rm,Damour:2010rp} and relaxions~\cite{Graham:2015cka,Flacke:2016szy}, or at loop level from axions~\cite{Kim:2022ype,Beadle:2023flm,Kim:2023pvt}. Terrestrial experiments, using techniques such as atomic spectroscopy~\cite{Arvanitaki:2014faa,VanTilburg:2015oza,Hees:2016gop,Zhang:2022ewz} and optomechanical systems~\cite{Stadnik:2014tta,Stadnik:2015xbn,Branca:2016rez,Campbell:2020fvq,Vermeulen:2021epa}, have pushed the measurement sensitivity of the fine-structure constant to unprecedented levels.


Recent advancements in astrophysical spectroscopic measurements have made it possible to test the fine-structure constant at the Galactic Center~\cite{Hees:2020gda}. Although astrophysical observations currently lack the resolution of well-controlled terrestrial experiments, the expected signals could be significantly enhanced due to the denser background of ultralight bosons at the Galactic Center. Mechanisms such as superradiance~\cite{Penrose:1971uk,ZS,Brito:2015oca} or relaxation driven by self-interactions~\cite{Budker:2023sex,Gan:2023swl} can lead to the formation of dense boson clouds around compact objects, with field amplitudes potentially reaching values as high as $\sim 10^{16}$\,GeV—close to the grand unification scale~\cite{Chen:2022kzv,Chen:2023vkq,Ayzenberg:2023hfw}. As a result, the vicinity of black holes (BHs) offers a powerful testing ground for ultralight bosons within corresponding mass ranges.

In this work, we propose using spectroscopic measurements of stars orbiting the supermassive BH (SMBH) Sgr A$^*$ at the center of the Milky Way to test for the existence of axions and scalars with quadratic couplings to electromagnetic fields. For axions, the field value may saturate at its decay constant, leading to well-predicted variations in the fine-structure constant around Sgr A$^*$, with a maximum amplitude of $\delta \alpha_{\rm EM}/\alpha_{\rm EM} \sim 10^{-7}$~\cite{Gruzinov:2016hcq,Baryakhtar:2020gao,Omiya:2022gwu}. Additionally, we explore the scenario of axion DM forming a soliton core in the Galactic Center, with a field value of approximately $10^{11}$\,GeV. The numerous S-stars, with their diverse spectral lines, form an effective network for coherent spectroscopic measurements, greatly improving sensitivity to coherent oscillations induced by the ultralight boson background.



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Quadratic Scalar Couplings to Photons}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

As pseudoscalar particles, axions typically couple linearly to parity-odd operators, such as the axion-gluon coupling $\propto \phi\,G_{\mu\nu} \tilde{G}^{\mu\nu}$ and the axion-photon coupling $\propto \phi\, F_{\mu\nu} \tilde{F}^{\mu\nu}$. Here, $\phi$ represents the boson field, $G_{\mu\nu}/\tilde{G}^{\mu\nu}$ and $F_{\mu\nu}/\tilde{F}^{\mu\nu}$ are the field strength tensors and their duals for gluons and photons, respectively. However, at the one-loop level, quadratic interactions with the photon kinetic term can be generated~\cite{Kim:2022ype,Beadle:2023flm,Kim:2023pvt}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{EM}} =  \frac{C_\gamma}{4} \frac{\phi^2}{f_\phi^2} F_{\mu\nu} F^{\mu\nu},\label{eq:aaFF}
\end{equation}
where $f_\phi$ is the decay constant of the axion, and $C_\gamma$ is a dimensionless coefficient resulting from the breaking of the axion’s shift symmetry. For KSVZ-like QCD axion models, whose shift symmetry is broken down to a discrete symmetry, $C_\gamma \approx -3 \times 10^{-5}$ has been calculated from diagrams involving pions and nucleons in the loop~\cite{Kim:2022ype,Beadle:2023flm,Kim:2023pvt}. Alternatively, interactions with ALPs can arise from either a QCD-like breaking to discrete shift symmetry or a breaking at ultraviolet scales, leading to a model-dependent value of $C_\gamma$~\cite{Beadle:2023flm}.

In addition to axions, quadratic couplings to photons naturally arise for scalar fields under symmetries such as $\mathbb{Z}_2$, parameterized as $\mathcal{L} \supset \phi^2 {F_{\mu\nu} F^{\mu\nu}}/{4\Lambda^2}$, where $\Lambda$ denotes the ultraviolet scale~\cite{Stadnik:2014tta,Stadnik:2015kia,Stadnik:2015xbn,Stadnik:2016zkf,Hees:2018fpg,Hees:2019nhn,Masia-Roig:2022net,Bouley:2022eer,Banerjee:2022sqg,Gan:2023wnp,Brax:2023udt}. Comparing this with Eq.~(\ref{eq:aaFF}), the coupling effectively replaces $C_\gamma/f_\phi^2$ with $1/\Lambda^2$. This study will not delve into linear couplings, as tests of the fifth force and equivalence principle have already imposed stringent constraints on such interactions~\cite{Berge:2017ovy,Hees:2018fpg}. These constraints can be circumvented by quadratic couplings, which mediate fifth forces at loop levels. Consequently, when the scalar field does not constitute the majority of DM, the leading constraint arises from energy loss in Supernova (SN) 1987A, setting a bound of $\Lambda = f_\phi / |C_\gamma|^{1/2} \geq 3$\,TeV~\cite{Olive:2007aj,Cox:2024oeb,Gan:2025nlu}.




When compared to the standard electromagnetic kinetic term, $\mathcal{L} \supset -{F_{\mu\nu} F^{\mu\nu}}/{4}$, axion couplings in Eq.~(\ref{eq:aaFF}), and similar quadratic scalar couplings, introduce measurable shifts in the electromagnetic fine-structure constant, $\delta \alpha_{\text{EM}}$. These shifts are expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\delta \alpha_{\text{EM}}}{\alpha_{\text{EM}}} \simeq C_\gamma\frac{\phi^2}{f_\phi^2} \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{\phi^2}{\Lambda^2},\label{eq:deltaalpha}
\end{equation}
assuming the deviation remains substantially smaller than the original value of $\alpha_{\text{EM}}$.






%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Fine-Structure Constant Oscillations at the Galactic Center}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Ultralight scalars, with a Compton wavelength exceeding the horizon size of a BH, can form quasi-bound clouds around the BH. These clouds, similar to hydrogen atoms, are characterized by quantum numbers $(n, \ell, m)$, representing the principal, orbital angular, and azimuthal numbers, respectively~\cite{Detweiler:1980uk}. The size of the cloud is governed by the gravitational fine-structure constant $\alpha_G \equiv G_{\rm N} M_{\rm BH} \mu$, where $G_{\rm N}$ is Newton’s constant, $M_{\rm BH}$ is the BH mass, and $\mu$ is the scalar mass. The rapid rotation of BHs can select specific bound states, transitioning them from decaying states due to the horizon to exponentially growing modes~\cite{Penrose:1971uk,ZS,Detweiler:1980uk,Cardoso:2005vk,Dolan:2007mj,Brito:2015oca}. This phenomenon, known as superradiance, occurs when the horizon rotates faster than the angular phase velocity of the states, parameterized by:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\alpha_G}{m} < \frac{a_J}{2\left(1+\sqrt{1-a_J^2}\right)},
\label{eq:SRC}
\end{equation}
where $a_J \leq 1$ is the dimensionless BH spin parameter. Exponential growth for minimally coupled scalars can continue until it significantly impacts the BH spin, with the total scalar cloud mass, $M_{\rm cloud}$, reaching up to approximately $10\%$ of the BH mass~\cite{Brito:2014wla,East:2017ovw,Herdeiro:2021znw}, and scalar field values approaching $\sim 10^{16}$\,GeV. This positions boson clouds outside Kerr BHs as potential testing grounds for exploring ultraviolet physics~\cite{Chen:2022kzv,Chen:2023vkq,Ayzenberg:2023hfw,Lyu:2025lue}.

On the other hand, when the axion decay constant is below $\sim 10^{16}$\,GeV,  
%\bzy{This may hint a causal relation between this and GUT physics?}, 
strong self-interactions from the cosine axion potential $V \approx -\mu^2 f_\phi^2 \cos(\phi/f_\phi)$ become crucial in terminating the exponential growth before the BH spins down~\cite{Arvanitaki:2010sy,Yoshino:2012kn,Gruzinov:2016hcq,Fukuda:2019ewf,Baryakhtar:2020gao,Omiya:2020vji,Omiya:2022mwv,Omiya:2022gwu,Collaviti:2024mvh,Takahashi:2024fyq,Aurrekoetxea:2024cqd,Witte:2024drg,Miller:2025yyx,Xie:2025npy}. More explicitly, in the Newtonian limit $\alpha_G \ll 1$, self-interactions lead to a saturated state of both the $(2,1,1)$ and $(3,2,2)$ modes, where weak leakage of axion waves towards infinity, resulting from the annihilation of two $(3,2,2)$ modes, balances the BH’s superradiant injection~\cite{Gruzinov:2016hcq,Baryakhtar:2020gao}. Since $(2,1,1)$ is the fastest-growing and dominant mode in this saturated state, we will focus on it in this study. Its wavefunction can be expressed as~\cite{Gruzinov:2016hcq,Baryakhtar:2020gao}:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align} \phi &= \phi_0^{\rm max}\, R(r) \cos \left(\mu t - \varphi+\Delta_0\right) \sin \theta \, ,\label{eq:phiwave} \\
     R (r) &\equiv e^{1-\frac{\alpha_G^2 r}{2 r_g}}\,\frac{\alpha_G^2 r}{2 r_g} \, , \\
 \phi_0^{\rm max} &\approx 0.07\, \alpha_G^2 M_{\rm pl}  \sqrt{\frac{M_{\rm cloud}}{M_{\rm BH}}} 
\approx 0.5\, \alpha_G f_\phi.\label{eq:phimax}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Here, we use the Cartesian spherical coordinates $(t,r,\theta,\varphi)$ where $\theta=0$ corresponds to the BH spin direction, $\Delta_0$ is an initial phase, $R(r) \leq 1$ is the normalized radial wavefunction, $r_g \equiv G_N M_{\rm BH}$ is the gravitational radius of the BH, and $M_{\rm pl} \equiv (1/G_N)^{1/2}$ is the Planck mass. $\phi_0^{\rm max}$ denotes the maximum field amplitude within the cloud, located at $r = 2\, r_g/\alpha_G^2, \theta = \pi/2$. The first approximation in Eq.~(\ref{eq:phimax}) derives from integrating the energy density in the Newtonian limit, while the second approximation accounts for the saturation state, neglecting the minor $a_J$-dependence~\cite{Gruzinov:2016hcq,Baryakhtar:2020gao}.

By substituting the amplitude of the wavefunction from Eq.~(\ref{eq:phiwave}), $\phi_0 \equiv \phi_0^{\rm max} R(r) \sin \theta$, into Eq.~(\ref{eq:deltaalpha}), we can derive the oscillation amplitude of the fine-structure constant, $\delta \alpha_{\text{EM}}^0/\alpha_{\text{EM}}$. The normalized wavefunction amplitude, $\phi_0/\phi_0^{\rm max}$ for $\alpha_G = 0.03$, is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:2DalphaEM}. 

