As the cloud’s radial wavefunction decays exponentially at larger distances ($r \gg 1/(\mu \alpha_G)$), most segments of a highly eccentric orbit within a single period exhibit suppressed oscillations in $\alpha_{\rm EM}$ compared to those at periastron. Nevertheless, measurements at larger distances remain highly relevant for probing ultralight axion DM, which forms a soliton core with a size $r_c = 4\,\text{pc} \times (10^{-18}\,\text{eV}/\mu)$ inversely proportional to its mass~\cite{Schive:2014dra}. Within the core, the axion field oscillates coherently with a nearly constant amplitude $\phi_0^c \approx 2 \times 10^{11}$,GeV, independent of $\mu$, as long as $f_\phi$ is much larger than $\phi_0^c$, ensuring that contact self-interactions are negligible~\cite{Schive:2014dra,Yuan:2020xui}.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{S-star Spectroscopic Measurements}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
We focus on the SMBH Sgr A$^*$ at the center of the Milky Way, around which approximately $40$ orbiting S-stars~\cite{Gillessen_2017} provide a unique opportunity for spectroscopic measurements to test variations in the fine-structure constant. The mass of Sgr A$^*$ has been precisely determined to be $M_{\rm BH}=4.30 \times 10^6 M_\odot$, with an uncertainty of $\pm0.25\%$, based on these stellar orbits~\cite{GRAVITY:2021xju}. Although the spin value and direction of Sgr A$^*$ have not been definitively established, horizon-scale observations from the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) suggest rapid rotation and a nearly face-on inclination~\cite{EventHorizonTelescope:2022wkp,EventHorizonTelescope:2022urf}. To address the uncertainty in spin and validate the Newtonian approximation for the wavefunction in Eqs.~(\ref{eq:phiwave},\ref{eq:phimax}), we consider a conservative range of $0.025 \leq \alpha_G \leq 0.1$, which meets the superradiance condition (\ref{eq:SRC}) for $a_J \geq 0.5$. The lower limit of $\alpha_G$ ensures that the superradiant timescale, $\tau_{\rm SR} \approx 24/(a_J \alpha_G^8 \mu)$~\cite{Detweiler:1980uk}, is shorter than $10^{10}$\,years, consistent with the timing of the past merger~\cite{Wang:2024lzu} and the age of the universe~\cite{Planck:2018vyg}. This range of $\alpha_G$ translates to a scalar mass $\mu \equiv \alpha_G/(G_N M_{\rm BH})$ within $[7.8 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{eV}, 3.1 \times 10^{-18}\,\text{eV}]$, resulting in an oscillation period for the quadratically coupling signal $T \approx 2\pi/(2\mu)$ varying from $11$ to $44$\,minutes. The extreme mass ratio between Sgr A$^*$ and the S-stars, combined with their considerable separation of $\gg 10^3\,r_g$, ensures that the superradiance effect is robust against tidal forces~\cite{Arvanitaki:2009fg,Baumann:2018vus,Cardoso:2020hca}.

Since the scalar cloud exhibits an anisotropic wavefunction (\ref{eq:phiwave}), with its angular momentum aligned with the BH spin axis, it is crucial to analyze the orbits of S-stars within the BH reference frame. We follow the procedure outlined in Ref.~\cite{Grould:2017bsw} to transform the orbital motion into the BH frame, as detailed in Supplemental Material. This transformation requires specifying the BH's inclination angle $i_{\rm BH}$ and the spin projection position angle $\Omega_{\rm BH}$. We adopt values of $i_{\rm BH} = 155\pm 5^\circ$ and $\Omega_{\rm BH} = 177\pm25^\circ$, derived from fits to hotspot motion~\cite{GRAVITY:2023avo}, which are consistent with previous results from GRAVITY and the EHT~\cite{2018,EventHorizonTelescope:2022wkp}. The orbit of S0-2/S2, one of the closest and best-measured S-stars, is illustrated by a white line in Fig.~\ref{fig:2DalphaEM}, based on these fiducial values.


Each star exhibits absorption lines resulting from atomic transitions in its atmosphere, with frequencies determined by the atom’s energy levels, which depend on the value of $\alpha_{\rm EM}$~\cite{Hees:2020gda}. For stars orbiting near an SMBH, these spectral lines experience shifts due to both Doppler effects and gravitational redshift arising from the SMBH’s gravitational potential. In the case of S-stars, whose orbits have Keplerian periods exceeding $10$ years, these Doppler and gravitational effects can be well-characterized and fit, leaving any residual shifts~\cite{Chu:2017gvt,Do:2019txf,Hees:2020gda} as a potential signature of spectral line oscillations on much shorter timescales. More explicitly, the residual deviation $\delta \lambda_j$ of the $k$-th spectral line in terms of its original wavelength $\lambda_j$ relates to shifts in $\alpha_{\rm EM}$ as follows:
\be \frac{\delta \lambda_j}{\lambda_j} \approx -k_{\alpha, j} \frac{\delta \alpha_{\rm EM}}{\alpha_{\rm EM}}, \ee
where $k_{\alpha, j}$ quantifies the sensitivity of the $j$-th transition to $\alpha_{\rm EM}$, detailed in Ref.~\cite{Hees:2020gda}. We focus on the fast oscillation component of $\delta \alpha_{\rm EM}$ with frequency $2\mu$, which, due to its oscillatory nature, makes the signal more distinct compared to the non-oscillatory part of the wavefunction that varies over the same period as the orbit~\cite{Yuan:2022nmu}.



For this sensitivity analysis, we first consider recent spectroscopic observations of S0-2/S2 from the Gemini Near-Infrared Integral Field Spectrometer (NIFS), focusing on its closest approach to the SMBH in 2017 and 2018~\cite{Do:2019txf}, comprising a total of $25$ individual observations, each lasting approximately $3$ hours.
The raw data consist of frames with $10$- or $15$-minute integration times, which were subsequently averaged on a daily basis (see the Supplemental Material of Ref.~\cite{Do:2019txf}). In our analysis, we treat each $10$- and $15$-minute frame individually. To estimate the typical spectroscopic uncertainty per frame, we analyze the set of frames corresponding to a single night of NIFS observations. We then generate mock residuals for the hydrogen line over the 2017–2018 epoch, corresponding to the data reported in Ref.~\cite{Do:2019txf}, in the form $\delta \lambda_j / \lambda_j$, with $k_{\alpha, j} \approx 2$ and a median uncertainty of $10^{-4}$ per frame. Based on this, we estimate the sensitivity to $|C_\gamma|$, as detailed in the Supplemental Material and illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:constraints}, for $\alpha_G < 0.05$—a limit imposed by the observation cadence. The resulting exclusion curve shows a modest dip below $|C_\gamma| = 1$, primarily due to the $\alpha_G^2$ suppression in Eq.~(\ref{eq:phimax}) at lower masses and the increasing distance from the $\phi_0^{\rm max}$ location at higher masses. The black line indicates the sensitivity using fiducial values of $i_{\rm BH}$ and $\Omega_{\rm BH}$, while the gray band reflects variations in these parameters due to measurement uncertainties~\cite{GRAVITY:2023avo}, which play only a minor role in the overall exclusion. For comparison, we also show previous constraints from SN1987A, which place a lower bound on $\Lambda \equiv f_\phi / |C_\gamma|^{1/2} \geq 3\,\text{TeV}$~\cite{Olive:2007aj}, assuming $f_\phi = 10^{12}$\,GeV and scaling linearly for other values.




