Finally, we consider the projective constraints on the axion as the dominant component of DM. The parsec-scale soliton core and the large field of view of the telescope enable the simultaneous observation of approximately $40$ stars within the core for $\mu \leq 10^{-18}$\,eV. The coherent nature of the core facilitates a network of correlated spectroscopic measurements, where each star provides multiple spectral lines that can be analyzed collectively to enhance constraints on $\alpha_{\rm EM}$ oscillations.

For comparison, we also present constraints from terrestrial experiments on fine-structure constant variations, shown as solid lines, including atomic clocks~\cite{VanTilburg:2015oza,Zhang:2022ewz,Hees:2016gop,Kennedy:2020bac,Filzinger:2023zrs,Sherrill:2023zah} and fifth force/equivalence principle violation (EPV) tests~\cite{Wagner:2012ui,Berge:2017ovy,Hees:2018fpg}. These EPV bounds have recently been refined to incorporate momentum-dependent effects arising from couplings to nucleons~\cite{Banerjee:2025dlo,Gue:2025nxq}. The future projection from HISPEC/MODHIS, assuming a setup similar to Fig.~\ref{fig:constraints} but incorporating simultaneous measurements of $40$ late-type stars, is expected to achieve comparable sensitivity at higher frequencies, providing complementary data under a conservative profile. Constraints from SN1987A~\cite{Springmann:2024ret}, nuclear parameter oscillations~\cite{Kim:2022ype,Banerjee:2023bjc,Madge:2024aot,Fuchs:2024xvc}, neutron electric dipole moment (nEDM) oscillations~\cite{Abel:2017rtm,Roussy:2020ily,Schulthess:2022pbp}, nuclear EPV tests~\cite{Gue:2025nxq}—all induced by axion-gluon couplings—are shown as dashed lines. Additionally, cosmological constraints from Big Bang nucleosynthesis~\cite{Blum:2014vsa,Bouley:2022eer} and limits arising from nucleon environment backreaction~\cite{Hook:2017psm,Zhang:2021mks,Balkin:2022qer,Gomez-Banon:2024oux,Kumamoto:2024wjd} provide further restrictions on the parameter space.









%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Discussion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
In the Galactic Center, the vicinity of an SMBH can host a high density of ultralight bosons. Two notable examples include: (1) BH superradiance, which can exponentially amplify a boson condensate bound to the BH, driving the field value to saturate the decay constant for axions, and (2) ultralight DM, which can form a soliton core. Due to the high field values in these scenarios, observables arising from axion interactions can be significantly enhanced compared to those from terrestrial DM detection. This makes the system particularly suitable for probing couplings generated from loop-level interactions, such as quadratic axion/scalar couplings to photons. In this study, we simulate precise spectroscopic measurements of S-stars orbiting the SMBH Sgr A$^*$ to investigate these couplings, focusing on axion profiles arising from either a superradiant cloud or a soliton core. The sensitivity of current data indicates that a significant portion of the parameter space can already be constrained, while near-future upgrades to observational capabilities promise deeper exploration of these regions.


While our study primarily employs a Newtonian wavefunction for the superradiant ground mode $(2,1,1)$, various other profiles can be considered. For example, at larger values of $\alpha$, relativistic corrections become crucial, causing the higher mode $(3,2,2)$ to dominate in cloud mass and size, thereby allowing more S-stars to probe its extent~\cite{Omiya:2022gwu}. Additionally, bound gravitational atoms formed from the relaxation of DM waves exhibit a spherical $(1,0,0)$ mode~\cite{Banerjee:2019epw,Budker:2023sex,Gan:2023swl}. Gravitational molecules form outside binaries of comparable mass, corotating with the binary system~\cite{Ikeda:2020xvt,Liu:2021llm,Bamber:2022pbs,Guo:2023lbv,Aurrekoetxea:2023jwk,Guo:2024iye}. These scenarios reveal complex interactions between boson condensates and BHs across different mass ranges, potentially leading to phenomena such as bosenova~\cite{Eby:2016cnq,Levkov:2016rkk,Budker:2023sex,Gan:2023swl,Takahashi:2024fyq,Aurrekoetxea:2024cqd}. Spectroscopic measurements could serve as a tool to visualize these dynamics, effectively capturing the evolution of the boson wavefunction through tomography.


A broad range of observations can be complementary, and synergistic approaches can elucidate both the macroscopic and microscopic characteristics of the axion/scalar field. Axions with linear coupling to photons can alter the orientation of linear polarization, and EHT observations can probe the fundamental parameter $c \equiv g_{a\gamma} f_\phi$ once the cloud saturates, where $g_{a\gamma}$ represents the linear axion-photon coupling constant~\cite{Chen:2019fsq,Yuan:2020xui,Chen:2021lvo,Chen:2022oad,Ayzenberg:2023hfw,Wang:2023eip}. A polarimetry-spectroscopy network could thus explore the ultraviolet origins of axions, complementing spin measurements that exclude large decay constants~\cite{Arvanitaki:2010sy,Arvanitaki:2014wva,Brito:2014wla,Cardoso:2018tly,Davoudiasl:2019nlo,Unal:2020jiy,Cheng:2022jsw,Saha:2022hcd,Guo:2024dqd,Guo:2025dkx}. The cloud’s morphology can be further calibrated through observations of purely gravitational effects, measuring the cloud mass~\cite{Cunha:2019ikd,Chen:2022nbb,Chen:2022kzv,Shen:2023kkm,GRAVITY:2023cjt,Khalaf:2024nwc,GRAVITY:2024tth}.




