\begin{abstract}
We study a sequential contextual decision-making problem in which certain covariates are missing but can be imputed using a pre-trained AI model. From a theoretical perspective, we analyze how the presence of such a model influences the regret of the decision-making process. We introduce a novel notion called \textit{``model elasticity''}, which quantifies the sensitivity of the reward function to the discrepancy between the true covariate and its imputed counterpart. This concept provides a unified way to characterize the regret incurred due to model imputation, regardless of the underlying missingness mechanism. More surprisingly, we show that under the missing at random (MAR) setting, it is possible to sequentially calibrate the pre-trained model using tools from orthogonal statistical learning and doubly robust regression. This calibration significantly improves the quality of the imputed covariates, leading to much better regret guarantees. Our analysis highlights the practical value of having an accurate pre-trained model in sequential decision-making tasks and suggests that model elasticity may serve as a fundamental metric for understanding and improving the integration of pre-trained models in a wide range of data-driven decision-making problems.
% We consider the sequential contextual decision-making problem under missing covariates but with the help of a pre-trained AI model from a theoretical perspective. We show that with the help of the pre-trained model imputation, no matter what kind of missing mechanism we face, to characterize the regret due to pre-trained model imputation, we could use a novel concept called ``model elasticity,'' which captures how sensitive the reward model is with respect to the difference between the true covariate and the imputed value. More surprisingly, we discover that under the missing at random (MAR) mechanism, we could sequentially calibrate the pre-trained model through techniques from orthogonal statistical learning and doubly robust regression to improve the performance of the pre-trained model and thus get a better regret rate. Our analysis implies that an accurate pre-trained model is of great help during the sequential decision-making process. We also believe that our new concept of ``model elasticity'' will also be widely used in other scenarios of sequential decision-making with pre-trained models. 

\textit{\small Key words: Artificial Intelligence, Sequential Decision-Making, Model Calibration, Contextual Bandits, Doubly Robust Regression, Statistical Learning} 
\end{abstract}
