	\subsection{Notation} \label{sec:notation}

	% Throughout this paper, we consider finite-dimensional Hilbert space.
	Let quantum systems (or quantum registers) $\A, \B, \ldots$ be associated with finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H}_{\A}, \mathcal{H}_{\B}, \ldots $, respectively.
	The quantum state of a system $\A$ is represented by a density operator $\rho_{\A}$, i.e., a positive semi-definite operator with unit trace on $\mathcal{H}_{\A}$.
	The set of quantum states on $\mathcal{H}_{\A}$ is denoted by $\mathcal{S}(\A)$.
	We denote by $\I_{\A}$ the identity operator on $\mathcal{H}_{\A}$, i.e., $\I_{\A} = \sum_{i} |i\rangle\langle i|_{\A}$ for any orthonormal basis $\{|i\rangle_{\A}\}_i$ of $\mathcal{H}_{\A}$.
	Sometimes we skip the subscript of the system name if we do not specify it.
	Given a bipartite state $\rho_{\A\B} \in \mathcal{S}(\A\B)$, the marginal state of system $\A$ is denoted by $\rho_{\A}$ by tracing out system $\B$: $\rho_{\A} = \Tr_{\B}\left[ \rho_{\A\B}\right] \coloneq \sum_{i} \I_{\A} \otimes \langle i |_{\B} \cdot \rho_{\A\B} \cdot \I_{\A}\otimes |i\rangle_{\B}$ for $\{|i\rangle_{\B}\}_i$ being any orthonormal basis  of $\mathcal{H}_{\B}$. 
	If we do not specify the subscript of $\Tr$, we mean to trace out all quantum systems.
	We denote by `$\dagger$' the complex conjugate transpose.

	For a scalar-valued function $f$ and a normal operator $A$ with spectral decomposition $A = \sum_i \lambda_i |i\rangle\langle i|$ whose spectrum $\{\lambda_i\}_i$ (denoted by $\spec (A)$) being in the domain of $f$, we define the operator $f(A)$ via functional calculus: $f(A) \coloneq \sum_i f(\lambda_i) |i\rangle \langle i|$.
	Since the negative real axis $\mathds{R}_{\leq 0}$ is not in the domain of the negative power function and the logarithmic function, we further assume that they are taken only on the support.
	The operator norm and trace norm of $A$ are denoted by $\|A\| \coloneq \sup_{i} \{|\lambda_i|\}$ and $\|A\|_1 \coloneq \Tr[|A|] = \sum_i |\lambda_i|$, respectively.

	We adopt L\"owner's partial order for the operator space; $A>B$ (resp.~$A\geq B$) means that $A-B > 0$ (resp.~$A- B\geq 0$) is a positive definite (resp.~positive semi-definite) operator.
	For a self-adjoint operator $X$ with spectral decomposition $X = \sum_i \lambda_i |i\rangle \langle i|$, we define the orthogonal projection onto its positive support by
	\[
	\left\{ X > 0 \right\} 
	\coloneq \sum_{i\colon \lambda_i>0}  |i\rangle \langle i|.
	\]
	Similarly, $\left\{ X \geq 0 \right\} 
	\coloneq \sum_{i\colon \lambda_i\geq0}  |i\rangle \langle i|$ and $\left\{ X = 0 \right\} 
	\coloneq \sum_{i\colon \lambda_i=0}  |i\rangle \langle i|$.

