\noindent \textbf{Ablation on the number of attention heads.} The number of attention heads is usually an essential parameter for models based on attention mechanisms. Thus, we also compared the effect of different choices of the number of heads on the six evaluation metrics of the model on the DMER dataset in both subject-dependent and subject-independent settings, shown in Figure \ref{fig6}. It can be seen that the model's performance first increases across subject-dependent and subject-independent settings as the number of attention heads increases. Then, the model achieves the best performance across all six evaluation metrics when the number of attention heads is equal to 4, after which the performance declines as the number of heads continues to increase. However, the number of attention heads has a relatively limited impact on the performance of the model.

\noindent \textbf{Ablation on the depth of transformer block.} The depth of end-to-end models is considered a crucial factor in determining their fitting ability. Therefore, we evaluate the model while varying the depth of the block on the DMER dataset to explore the impact of the depth of the transformer block. Figure \ref{fig7} shows the optimal classification performance was achieved when the depth of the block was 1, and as the depth increased, the classification accuracy tended to slightly decrease and leveled off as the depth continued to increase, as the greater the depth of the transformer block, the more likely it is to overfit the model.

