\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.5em}
We presented \textbf{Gradientsys}, a system for scheduling and orchestrating multiple LLM-powered agents to solve complex tasks. By combining the reasoning abilities of large language models with a robust multi-agent execution framework, Gradientsys uses a typed Model-Context Protocol and a dynamic ReAct-based planner to flexibly integrate new tools, dispatch tasks in parallel, and adapt plans as needed. Its architecture emphasizes transparency and efficiency, with live reasoning traces that improve trust and debuggability, while parallelism and local tool use reduce latency and API costs. In evaluations, Gradientsys outperformed single- and two-agent baselines, especially in tasks requiring specialized tool collaboration, generalizing approaches to broader scenarios and contrasting with code-first frameworks through dynamic AI planning. These results highlight hybrid AI orchestration—where an LLM serves as a coordinator of specialized agents—as a promising direction for developing more capable assistants.

% \textbf{Future Work:} There are several avenues to explore. One is incorporating learning: currently, Gradientsys's planner is prompt-driven; it could be fine-tuned on successful multi-agent plans to become even more reliable. Another is improving the cost-awareness in planning – integrating the \texttt{cost\_per\_1k} metadata more directly to have the LLM explicitly optimize for lower cost tool usage when possible (an interesting challenge in prompt design). We also plan to extend observability into a two-way interface, where a human overseer could give feedback mid-task (steering the agent if it's stuck – effectively human-AI collaboration). Finally, we aim to increase the set of tool types, including more complex actions (e.g. agents that can manipulate visual data or agents that can perform physical-world actions via IoT interfaces). Gradientsys's architecture is ready for these additions, and we believe it provides a strong foundation for the next generation of general AI agent systems that are extensible, efficient, and trustworthy.




