\section{Multiple Gamma and Sine Functions}\label{app:SF}
We follow the reference \cite{naru}. For $z,\omega_i\in\mathbb{C}$ all lying on the same side w.r.t. the imaginary axis of the complex plane, the Multiple Gamma function has the following integral representation 
\be\label{eq:GammaInt}
\Gamma_r(z|\ul\omega)=\exp\Big[\frac{\gamma}{r!}(-1)^r B_{rr}(z|\ul\omega)+\oint_\mathcal{C}\frac{\d k}{2\pi\i k}\ln(-k)\frac{\e^{-k z}}{\prod_{j=1}^r(1-\e^{-k \omega_j})}\Big]~,
\ee
where $\mathcal{C}$ is the Hankel contour, $\gamma$ the Euler-Mascheroni constant and $B_{rr}$ the multiple Bernoulli polynomials.

There is an identity \cite{FRIEDMAN2004362} converting the product of two Multiple Gamma functions into a multiple $\q$-Pochhammer, namely
\be\label{eq:GGqP}
\Gamma_r(x|1,\ul\alpha)
\Gamma_r(1-x|1,-\ul\alpha)=\frac{\e^{-\frac{\i\pi}{r!}(-1)^r B_{rr}(x|1,\ul\alpha)}}{(x;\ul\alpha)_\infty}~,\quad (x;\ul\alpha)_\infty\equiv (\e^{2\pi\i x};\e^{2\pi\i\alpha_1},\ldots, \e^{2\pi\i\alpha_{r-1}})_\infty~.
\ee

The Multiple Sine function for $\text{Re} \omega_j>0$, $\text{Re}\sum_j\omega_j>\text{Re}z>0$ has a similar integral representation
\be\label{eq:Srint}
S_r(z|\ul \omega)=\exp\Big[\pm(-1)^r\frac{\pi \i}{r!}B_{rr}(z|\ul\omega)-\oint_{\mathbb{R}\mp \i 0}\frac{\d k}{k}\frac{\e^{-k z}}{\prod_{j=1}^r(1-\e^{-k\omega_j})}\Big]~,
\ee
where the contour runs parallel to the real axis avoiding the origin. 


\section{Clavelli-Shapiro Trace Technique}\label{app:CS}


We offer a step-by-step proof of Clavelli-Shapiro trace formula, which cannot be easily found in the literature. We aim  to compute
\be
Z=\text{Tr} \ x^{\mb{L_0}} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)~, 
\ee
where $\mb{O}$ is some operator-valued function of $\mb{a}$, $\mb{a}^\dagger$, the usual annihilation/creation operators, and $\mb{L}_0\equiv\mb{a}^\dagger \mb{a}$ is the occupation number operator. They satisfy the non-trivial commutation relations
\be
[\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger]=1~,\quad [\mb{L}_0,\mb{a}]=-\mb{a}~,\quad [\mb{L}_0,\mb{a}^\dagger]=\mb{a}^\dagger~.
\ee
The Fock modules (over which we compute the trace) are generated by the vacua $\bra{0},\ket{0}$ such that
\be
\mb{a}\ket{0}=\bra{0}\mb{a}^\dagger=0~,
\ee
with $\braket{0}{0}=1$. Normalized basis states are given by
\be
\ket{n}=\frac{(\mb{a}^\dagger)^n}{\sqrt{n!}}\ket{0}~,\quad \bra{n}=\bra{0}\frac{(\mb{a})^n}{\sqrt{n!}}~,\quad n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}~.
\ee
We start noticing that $Z$ can be rewritten as a vacuum expectation value rather than as a trace by introducing another set of oscillators $\mb{b},\mb{b}^\dagger$ satisfying the very same commutation relations and commuting with $\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger$. Indeed
\bes
\begin{align}
\bra{0}\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}x^{\mb{L}_0}\mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\ket{0}=& \ \sum_{n,m\geq 0}\bra{0}\frac{\mb{a}^n\mb{b}^n}{n!} x^{\mb{L}_0} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\frac{(\mb{a}^\dagger)^m(\mb{b}^\dagger)^m}{m!}\ket{0}=\\
= & \ \sum_{n,m\geq 0}\bra{0}\frac{\mb{a}^n}{n!} x^{\mb{L}_0} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\frac{(\mb{a}^\dagger)^m}{m!}\ket{0}\bra{0}\mb{b}^n(\mb{b}^\dagger)^m\ket{0}=\\
= & \ \sum_{n,m\geq 0}\bra{0}\frac{\mb{a}^n}{n!} x^{\mb{L}_0} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\frac{(\mb{a}^\dagger)^m}{m!}\ket{0} m!\delta_{m,n}=\\
= & \  \sum_{n\geq 0}\bra{n} x^{\mb{L}_0} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{n}~.
\end{align}
\ees
We must now compute the l.h.s. in an alternative way by exploiting ordering and disentangling identities. Firstly, due to $[\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}]=-\mb{b}^\dagger$, $[\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger]=[\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{b}^\dagger]=0$, the BCH formula implies
\be
\e^{-\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{a}\e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}=\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger~,\quad \e^{-\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{a}^\dagger\e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}=\mb{a}^\dagger~,
\ee
and therefore
\be
\bra{0}\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}x^{\mb{L}_0} \mb{O}(\mb{a},\mb{a}^\dagger)\e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\ket{0}=\bra{0}\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}x^{\mb{L}_0} \e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{O}(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{0}~.
\ee
Now the fact that $\mb{L}_0$ is the grading/dilation operator for the $\mb{a}$, $\mb{a}^\dagger$ modes (or the BCH formula again) implies
\be
x^{-\mb{L}_0}\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}x^{\mb{L}_0}=\e^{x\mb{a}\mb{b}}~,
\ee
so
\be
\bra{0}\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}x^{\mb{L}_0} \e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{O}(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{0}=\bra{0}\e^{x\mb{a}\mb{b}} \e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{O}(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{0}~.
\ee
Using the commutation rule $[-\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger,x \mb{a}\mb{b}]=x(\mb{a}\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b}^\dagger \mb{b})$ and the BCH formula once more, namely
\be
\e^{-\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\e^{x\mb{a}\mb{b}}\e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}=\e^{x(\mb{a}\mb{b}+\mb{a}\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b}^\dagger \mb{b}+\mb{a}^\dagger \mb{b}^\dagger)}~,
\ee
we get
\be
\bra{0}\e^{x\mb{a}\mb{b}} \e^{\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger}\mb{O}(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{0}=\bra{0}\e^{x(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger)(\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b})} \mb{O}(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger)\ket{0}~.
\ee
In order to evaluate the exponential operator on the vacuum, we can follow at least two routes. The elegant one is based on the observation that $\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{b}^\dagger\equiv \mb{E}$, $\mb{a}\mb{b}\equiv \mb{F}$, $[\mb{E},\mb{F}]=\mb{a}^\dagger\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger\mb{b}+1\equiv \mb{H}$ close the ordinary $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ algebra, for which disentangling identities are known to reproduce \cite{PhysRevD.31.1988}
\be
\bra{0}\e^{x(\mb{F}+\mb{H}+\mb{E})}=\e^{-\ln(1-x)}\bra{0}\e^{\frac{x }{1-x}\mb{F}}~.
\ee
The less elegant one is based on brute force computation. In this case we can expand the exponential
\be
\e^{x(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger)(\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b})}=\sum_{n\geq 0}\frac{x^n}{n!}\sum_{k,j=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}(\mb{b}^\dagger)^k \mb{a}^{n-k}\binom{n}{j}(\mb{a}^\dagger)^j\mb{b}^{n-j}~,
\ee
and use the action of the oscillators on the Fock states, namely 
\be
\bra{n}(\mb{a}^\dagger)^m=  \sqrt{n(n-1)\cdots(n-m+1)}\bra{n-m}~,\quad 
\bra{n}\mb{a}^m=  \sqrt{(n+1)\cdots(n+m)}\bra{n+m}~,
\ee
to obtain
\be
\bra{0}\e^{x(\mb{a}+\mb{b}^\dagger)(\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b})}=\bra{0}\sum_{n,j\geq 0}x^j \binom{n+j}{j} \frac{(x\mb{a}\mb{b})^n}{n!}~.
\ee
The sum over $j$ (operator-independent part) is a known generating function ($|x|<1$)
\be
\sum_{j\geq 0}x^j \binom{n+j}{j}=\frac{1}{(1-x)^{n+1}}~.
\ee
 Finally, using
 \be
\e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}\mb{a}^\dagger\e^{-\mb{a}\mb{b}}=\mb{a}^\dagger+\mb{b}~,\quad \e^{\mb{a}\mb{b}}\mb{b}^\dagger\e^{-\mb{a}\mb{b}}=\mb{b}^\dagger+\mb{a}~,
\ee
we deduce the formula
\be
Z=\e^{-\ln(1-x)}\bra{0}\mb{O}\Big(\frac{\mb{a}}{1-x}+\mb{b}^\dagger,\mb{a}^\dagger-\frac{\mb{b}}{1-x^{-1}}\Big)\ket{0}~.
\ee

This derivation can be generalized to the case of multiple independent oscillators, both in the discrete case and in the continuous case whenever a suitable regularization scheme is employed to make sense of continuous products and singular integrals (e.g. via discretization, multiplicative integrals and deformed contours). We refer to \cite{Jimbo:1996ev} for a discussion in a related context. 



