\section{Toward AI-RAN}
\label{sec:ai-ran}
% \hl{Here we discuss the three pillars of AI-RAN, and the motivation behind it}

The transition towards AI-native mobile networks is 
% fundamentally transforming the design, deployment, and operation of \gls{ran}. As \gls{ai} continues to be integrated into network functions and network infrastructure, it becomes essential to establish a structured framework that defines how \gls{ai} interacts with \gls{ran} at different levels. 
% This transformation is 
being driven by three complementary research tracks within the AI-RAN Alliance: AI-for-RAN, AI-on-RAN, and AI-and-RAN. This section reviews the key principles behind each AI-RAN working group. We then analyzes how these efforts align with O-RAN ALLIANCE specification activities to establish the technical foundation for our converged architecture presented in Sec.~\ref{sec:architecture}.

\subsection{AI-RAN Principles}
\label{sec:principles}

\textbf{AI-for-RAN.} 
The first pillar, AI-for-RAN, applies \gls{ai} to enhance network automation, efficiency, and adaptability \cite{li2025rethinking}. 
%AI models assist in tasks such as spectrum management, interference mitigation, predictive traffic analysis, and dynamic resource allocation \cite{li2025rethinking}.
%By embedding \gls{ai} into network control processes, \gls{ran} operations can respond intelligently to fluctuating network conditions, optimizing performance across various scenarios.
AI-for-RAN enhances RAN management through data-driven decision-making, improving performance, reliability, and cost efficiency via automation and self-healing \cite{lin2023embracing, AI-RAN_Whitepaper_2024}. While this enables intelligent, self-optimizing networks, its scope remains limited to optimizing existing RAN functions without extending AI’s role beyond network operations~\cite{AI-RAN_Whitepaper_2024,polese2023understanding}.

\textbf{AI-on-RAN.}
Conversely, AI-on-RAN enables \gls{ai} and generative \gls{ai} applications to execute on the \gls{ran} infrastructure. These applications may or may not be related to cellular systems, but can leverage spare compute capacity and the geographically distributed nature of the \gls{ran} infrastructure. Traditional \gls{ran} deployments are over-provisioned for peak loads, therefore, by leveraging \gls{ai} during low \gls{ran} activity periods, operators can maximize infrastructure efficiency without compromising real-time cellular communication  \cite{ananthanarayanan2024distributed}. 
% to enhance user services and provide new capabilities across enterprise, consumer, and government applications. 
% AI-on-RAN transforms \gls{ran} into a distributed \gls{ai} computing platform for future wireless ecosystems. The key benefit of AI-on-RAN lies in its ability to unlock the latent compute resources of \gls{ran} infrastructure
This allows operators to provide AI-as-a-Service for diverse AI-driven applications while ensuring high-performance edge computing. By embedding \gls{ai} workloads within the \gls{ran}, AI-on-RAN brings AI close to end-users and thus reduces the latency and bandwidth overhead associated with sending data to centralized data centers, making it ideal for latency-sensitive applications that require real-time inference and decision making. Examples of AI-on-RAN workloads can include distributed AI training (e.g., federated learning), video processing and computer vision applications, or edge deployments of generative \gls{ai} solutions (e.g., a low-latency edge instance of an \gls{llm}), among others.
%For example, XR applications benefit from AI-on-RAN by utilizing edge compute resources to process complex rendering and prediction models in real time, enhancing user experience with minimal latency. Similarly, autonomous vehicle systems can perform sensor fusion and decision-making closer to the data source, improving safety and reliability. Federated learning applications also benefit from AI-on-RAN by enabling distributed \gls{ai} model training across multiple \gls{ran} nodes while maintaining data privacy and reducing communication overhead.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\textbf{AI-and-RAN.}
To enable infrastructure sharing across AI applications and \gls{ran} workloads, AI-and-RAN focuses on the coexistence of heterogeneous workloads on the same compute platform. 
Indeed, \gls{ai} and \gls{ran} workloads differ in terms of reliability, availability, and performance requirements. The \gls{ran} digital signal processing represents a non-elastic workload, where compute tasks need to be executed within specific timing constraints to ensure that the overall protocol stack can function as intended (e.g., for channel coding/decoding, scheduling, among others). At the same time, processing for a specific carrier configuration (bandwidth, number of MIMO streams) requires a fixed and predictable amount of resources. \gls{ai} and generative \gls{ai} inference or training solutions are more elastic, provided that enough resources are allocated to load the data structures associated with the AI models. 

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.95\linewidth]{images/challenges-v2.pdf}
        \setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{-.05cm}
    \setlength{\belowcaptionskip}{-.45cm}
    \caption{Challenges toward the integration of AI and RAN within the O-RAN architecture.}
    \label{fig:challenges}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{AI-RAN Orchestration Requirements.} 
Considering these requirements, the orchestration of AI-for/on-RAN requires (i) a holistic accounting of the underlying compute and networking resources, so as to dynamically track infrastructure availability; (ii) northbound \glspl{api} to AI and RAN developers, to expose available capacity with a high granularity and to enable submitting workloads to the system, thus enabling infrastructure monetization; (iii) an interface that allows operators to express requirements and intents for the AI-RAN orchestrator (e.g., priority level for \gls{ran} workloads, maximum latency for AI inference); (iv) a scheduling strategy that combines requests, intents, and available resources into actionable policies or allocations; and (v) a southbound interface that connects to the infrastructure for AI and RAN configuration and deployment, and also allows for preemption in case time-sensitive \gls{ran} workloads need to be prioritized. Figure~\ref{fig:SMOS-arch-1} captures these requirements in the end-to-end architecture.

