\section{Challenges and Open Issues Toward AI-RAN and O-RAN Convergence}
\label{sec:challenges}

Figure~\ref{fig:challenges} summarizes the main challenges toward effective AI-and-RAN orchestration and integration with O-RAN systems. These challenges arise from deploying \gls{ai} into \gls{oran}’s open and programmable architecture, which requires robust coordination between \gls{ai} and O-RAN-compliant network functions.

\textbf{Architecture and Workflows Challenges.} AI-RAN coexistence requires an evolution of the 3GPP and O-RAN architectures. In the control plane, the end-to-end management system needs to include an entity responsible for the coexistence between AI and RAN, which maps the functionalities of the orchestrator introduced in Fig.~\ref{fig:SMOS-arch-1} and in Sec.~\ref{sec:ai-ran}. This includes understanding what are the proper control and data exposure abstractions for such a task, what is the component of the network that can host such functionalities, and if there needs to be a hierarchical approach to orchestration. 

From a workflow point of view, there is a need for data, telemetry, control, and deployment pipelines, extending the O-RAN interfaces. This includes the definition of the southbound and northbound interfaces of the orchestrator, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:SMOS-arch-1} at a high level.
%
Southbound interfaces should support the continuous collection, pre-processing, and delivery of high-quality telemetry from distributed AI-RAN components, accommodating the latency requirements of different monitoring, inference, or control applications.
% These pipelines form the backbone for infrastructure monitoring, but can also be used for real-time inference and large-scale model training. 
% Such pipelines should . 
The design of these interfaces comes with the unique opportunity to enable open and systematic processes for the creation of large-scale datasets.
% , unique enablers of AI-RAN solutions. 
Finally, what \glspl{kpm} are better suited to support orchestrator tasks is an open question.

AI-RAN-specific procedures must be defined within the architectural framework to manage the lifecycle of \gls{ai} workloads. This implies regulating access to RAN resources, orchestrating deployment across nodes, and ensuring compliance with the operator's policies. To do so, current \gls{oran} interfaces (e.g., O1, O2, E2, A1) require targeted modifications to support the dual operation of RAN control loops and \gls{ai} workflows. 
% For instance, enhancements to the E2, O2, and O1 interfaces may be necessary to enable bidirectional exchange of \gls{ai} model parameters, metrics, and control signals, ensuring that \gls{ai} agents can dynamically interact with RAN functions while maintaining standardization and interoperability. 
Without these foundational elements, the AI-RAN vision cannot scale beyond isolated use cases into a fully integrated, intelligent network infrastructure.

\textbf{Orchestration Challenges.} From an infrastructure point of view, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:challenges}, an AI-RAN system includes a heterogeneous pool that combines compute with different \gls{ai} accelerators (e.g., \glspl{gpu}), storage, networking elements, radios, and spectrum, which the orchestrator needs to jointly take into account when allocating resources. RAN nodes exhibit diverse processing capabilities, also based on deployment location. Differences in computational power, memory, and latency requirements can lead to inefficiencies in workload distribution \cite{kundu2025ai, liu2022cloud}. The heterogeneity also extends to the workloads: as discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:ai-ran}, \gls{ai} and \gls{ran} often have different workload patterns and requirements. \gls{ai} itself can range from lightweight inference for real-time network optimization or value-added services to high-intensity model training. Moreover, the unpredictability of \gls{ai} workloads, and in particular that of large-scale training tasks, can introduce latency spikes that may negatively impact \gls{ran} performance \cite{kundu2023hardware, 3gpp_TR_38.843}. This complicates both monitoring, which needs to account for real-time variations in the workload intensity, and orchestration, which needs to combine multi-scale and proactive coexistence approaches. 

\textbf{Resource Allocation Challenges.} The resource allocation problem for the orchestrator needs to keep into account multiple factors including operators' priorities, the multi-dimensionality of the problem (encompassing a distributed infrastructure across different geographical locations with heterogeneous capabilities), and the potential presence of a hierarchical control structure with varying granularity, from a centralized orchestrator to distributed components. The orchestrator input also comes from different stakeholders (e.g., AI developers, RAN vendors, operators), whose orthogonal requirements may need to be harmonized and reconciled. 

\textbf{Use Case Challenges.} The AI-RAN orchestrator enables monetization strategies for next-generation wireless. These depend on identifying trade-offs, prioritization, and provisioning strategies to satisfy both AI and RAN tenants. To successfully cater to stakeholders representing AI-on-RAN use cases, 
the orchestrator northbound \gls{ai} \glspl{api} need to align with best practices in the \gls{ai} and cloud industries. 
The coexistence and monetization challenge also extends to energy efficiency. With \gls{ran} energy consumption being the highest operational expenditure for operators to date, it is important to adopt an energy-efficient and opportunistic approach to the acceleration of \gls{ai}. AI models are usually associated with high computational demands, in contrast to recent trends for low-power \gls{ran} components. Hardware acceleration offers potential mitigation but requires careful scheduling to ensure energy-efficient performance \cite{kundu2023hardware}.

Integrating third-party AI workloads also presents challenges to security, privacy, and access management. The orchestrator \glspl{api} and the end-to-end control and infrastructure management pipelines need to ensure accountability and tracking of security roles and privileges. 
One of the primary concerns is the deployment of \gls{ai} models or workloads which include vulnerabilities in the software stack that may affect the stability of the \gls{ran}. Given the critical nature of \gls{ran} operations, such attacks could lead to network congestion, degraded service quality, or even large-scale outages \cite{khan2023ai}.

In the next section, we introduce an architecture designed to address these challenges while adhering to the design principle of minimizing system complexity, and considering factors such as privacy and security.


