\section*{Introduction}
%A central focus of modern condensed matter physics has been the exploration and understanding of emergent phenomena in quantum materials, driven by the search for effective methods of their manipulation. These emergent phases of matter are governed by the intricate interplay and delicate balance between various energy scales associated with the relevant degrees of freedom. When two or more energy scales become similar in magnitude, different scenarios can arise. For example, one phase of matter may suppress the other competing order, as observed in high temperature superconducting cuprates, where charge and spin orders impede the establishment of superconductivity at even higher temperatures \cite{Fradkin2015Colloquium:Superconductors, Tranquada2013SpinsCuprates}. In other cases, strong fluctuations prevent the establishment of long-range order, as in the case of quantum paraelectric SrTiO$_3$ \cite{Nova2019MetastableSrTiO3, Li2019TerahertzSrTiO3}. External stimuli such as pressure, strain, and chemical doping have been extensively employed to tip the balance between these energy scales and thereby to gain an active control over the functional properties of quantum materials. In this respect, the proximity to phase boundaries, which amplifies susceptibility to external perturbations, provides intriguing ways of controlling these materials by exploiting equilibrium fluctuations near phase transitions \cite{Zong2021}. 

%A central focus of modern condensed matter physics has been the exploration and understanding of emergent phenomena in quantum materials, driven by the search for effective methods of their manipulation. These emergent phases of matter are governed by the delicate interplay of different microscopic degrees of freedom determined by their relative energy scales and their generally strong coupling. When two or more of these energy scales become comparable, typically close to a phase boundary, different scenarios can arise. For example, one phase of matter may suppress a competing order, as observed in high temperature superconducting cuprates, where charge and spin orders impede the establishment of long-range superconductivity. \cite{Fradkin2015Colloquium:Superconductors, Tranquada2013SpinsCuprates}. In other cases,  strong-fluctuations driven by the near degeneracy of two distinct phases, can actively suppress the emergence of any long-range order altogether.  One example is the oxide material SrTiO$_3$, where strong quantum fluctuations of the ionic motions prevent the formation of long-range ferroelectric ordering at any temperature \cite{Miiller1991IndicationSrTi03}.  The most prominent example of a fluctuating ground state in modern condensed matter physics are the underdoped cuprates. Here, antiferromagnetic fluctuations are believed to be the origin of the pseudogap phase  \cite{Schmalian1998WeakSuperconductors, Sedrakyan2010PseudogapFluctuations, Ye2019HubbardFluctuations}.

