\begin{abstract}
    Tokenization is used almost universally by modern language models, enabling efficient text representation using multi-byte or multi-character tokens.
    % These models are typically invoked to autoregressively complete a text prompt by tokenizing the prompt, sampling more tokens to continue the tokenized prompt, and detokenizing the result.
    However, prior work has shown that 
    % \alisa{this is too weak bc we know this to be true, and to me it sounds like you're about to refute it. one easy fix could be ``found'' $\rightarrow$ ``shown''} 
    tokenization can introduce distortion into the model's generations.
    For example, users are often advised not to end their prompts with a space because it prevents the model from including the space as part of the next token.
    This Prompt Boundary Problem (PBP) also arises in languages such as Chinese and in code generation, where tokens often do not line up with syntactic boundaries.
    Additionally mismatching tokenizers often hinder model composition and interoperability.
    For example, it is not possible to directly ensemble models with different tokenizers due to their mismatching vocabularies.
    %  \alisa{idk this is weird because the model isn't doing merges} \jon{How's this?} \alisa{much better!}
    % While this heuristic is effective in English, the underlying problem continues to affect languages such as Chinese as well as code generation, settings where word and syntactic boundaries may not line up with token boundaries.
    To address these issues, we present an inference-time method to convert any autoregressive LM with a BPE tokenizer into a character-level or byte-level LM, \textit{without changing its generative distribution at the text level}. 
    % We present an optimal method to solve this \textit{Prompt Boundary Problem}, which is based on an efficient online algorithm for Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE).
    % This allows one to compute the next byte distribution conditioned on an arbitrary byte prefix, given only logit access to the original tokenizer-based model.
    % This procedure can be applied iteratively to convert any autoregressive LM with a BPE tokenizer into a character-level or byte-level LM, \textit{without changing the generative distribution at the text level}. 
