Tokenization is a crucial component of nearly all modern language models: it allows them to consume and produce arbitrary streams of text using only finite vocabularies. 
The vast majority of tokenizers in use today, such as those based on Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) \citep{sennrich-etal-2016-neural} or Unigram \citep{kudo2018SentencePiece}, feature tokens spanning multiple bytes or characters, allowing them to  represent text more efficiently than purely byte-level or character-level tokenization \citep{clark-etal-2022-canine, xue-etal-2022-byt5, wang-etal-2024-mambabyte}. 
% \nascomment{maybe add cites for those alternatives} \alisa{added}.
%The primary goal of these modern tokenizers is to achieve the best encoding efficiency with a finite vocabulary size of choice.

% Longer tokens do have their downsides, however.
% Users generally expect language models to operate on strings, consuming a prompt as a string and producing a useful continuation thereof.
% The general procedure to approximate this with a tokenized language model is to: (1) apply the tokenizer to the prompt, (2) feed the resulting token sequence to the language model, and (3)
% translate the generated continuation back into text. 
% % For the most part, tokenizers are able to maintain the illusion that this is indeed the case.
% For the most part, this process happens transparently to the user, but under certain circumstances it can introduce distortion to the language model's completions.

% The typical way this can happen is when the user prompts using only a portion of a common token.

% Longer tokens, however, can distort the sampling distribution if the boundary between the prompt and its completion is not carefully handled.
Users of LMs are generally unaware of the tokenization and expect LMs to operate on strings, consuming a prompt as a string and producing a useful string completion thereof.
Tokenized LMs approximate this by \textit{(i)} encoding the text as a sequence of tokens, \textit{(ii)} feeding the resulting sequence to the language model, and \textit{(iii)}
decoding the generated token sequence back into text.
% Users do not understand tokens, so we must perform translation to use the language model.
More precisely, let \(\operatorname{prompt} \in \Sigma^*\) be a string of arbitrary length over some alphabet \(\Sigma\), and let \(\operatorname{encode}\colon \Sigma^* \to V^*\) and \(\operatorname{decode} \colon V^* \to \Sigma^*\) represent the translation between strings and token sequences over a vocabulary \(V\).  
To complete the prompt, a typical scheme is to sample  from the distribution, 
% Let \(V\) represent the language model's vocabulary and \(V^*\) be the set of all sequences of elements of \(V\).
% \[\Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{encode}\del{P}} =\prod_{i=k+1}^n \Pr{t_i \mid \sbr{t_1, \ldots, t_k} = \operatorname{encode}\del{P}, t_{k+1}, \ldots, t_{i-1}}.\]
\begin{equation}
    % \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{prompt}}} \;\;\triangleq \;\;
    \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \sbr{t_1, \ldots, t_k} = \operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{prompt}}} \;, \label{eq:cond-naive}
\end{equation}
where \(\operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{prompt}}\) is the tokenization of the prompt, which in this example has length \(k\).
% \alisa{what is the triangle equal symbol LOL. if this were someone else's paper, it might be where I give up :)}
% \begin{equation}
%     \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{prompt}}} = \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{prompt}} \sqsubseteq \sbr{t_1, \ldots, t_k}}\label{eq:cond-naive}
% \end{equation}
Note that sampling from this distribution can be done very conveniently by following the three steps above when the model has an autoregressive structure, i.e., 
% \[\Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n} \;\;=\;\; \prod_{i=1}^n \Pr{t_i\mid t_1, \ldots, t_{i-1}}\;,\]
\[\Pr{t_{k+1}, \ldots, t_n \mid t_1,\ldots,t_k} \;\;=\;\; \prod_{i=k+1}^n \Pr{t_i\mid t_1, \ldots, t_{i-1}}\;,\]
which is used to sample the completion from $\Pr{t_{k+1}, \ldots, t_n\mid t_1,\ldots,t_k}$ given the tokenized prompt $[t_1,\ldots,t_k]$.
We then return \(\operatorname{decode}\del{t_1, \ldots, t_n}\) to the user.
For the most part, this process happens transparently
% \alisa{not sure why the transparent part is emphasized here, I thought it would be more like ``for the most part this works''} \jon{it means the user doesn't need to think about tokenization}\alisa{oh. if it's transparent, doesn't mean it's seen by the user?} 
to the user, 
but under certain circumstances it can introduce distortion to the language model's completions, as we are about to explain.
