\textbf{The Prompt Boundary Problem (PBP).} 
% \nascomment{nice name; strongly suggest referencing this name in the abstract rather than the oblique and mysterious ``distortion''} 
To be precise, \cref{eq:cond-naive} introduces distortion whenever 
% One situation where \cref{eq:cond-naive} distorts the completion is when 
the prompt ends on a prefix of what could otherwise be a single token. 
% \alisa{isn't this always the cause of PBP?}
More concretely, consider \lm{Llama-3.2-1B} and suppose the user's prompt ends with the text \texttt{``becau''} (\texttt{["bec" = 17106, "au" = 2933]} as tokens): 
% \alisa{removed ellipticals bc you're already saying it ends with this string, I interpreted it to mean it literally ends with elliticals followed by ``becau''}
% \textcolor{orange}{[SO: let's write what byte-sequence 17106 and 2933 correspond to. I am guessing "be" and "cau"...]}
The user most likely expects the continuation to begin with \texttt{``se''} (\texttt{325}) since ``because'' is a common word.
However during training, the model has only ever seen the word ``because'' represented as a single token (\texttt{11458}) and never as the sequence \texttt{[17106, 2933, 325]}.
Accordingly, the actual next token \lm{Llama-3.2-1B} predicts is \texttt{``z''} (\texttt{89}) which, while plausible in some scenarios, is an arguably unlikely continuation representing an artifact of tokenization.
While this example may seem contrived at first glance, there are many situations where this problem may arise (\cref{fig:examples} shows a few more examples):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item In languages that do not separate words with whitespace%
    % \footnote{This is sometimes referred to as \textit{Scriptio continua}.}
    , such as Chinese and Japanese, tokens can span multiple words, so this issue can arise even when the prompt ends with a complete word.
    \item Any tokenizer that features multi-word tokens, which can bring gains in encoding efficiency \citep{gee-etal-2023-multi,kumar-thawani-2022-bpe,liu-etal-2025-superbpe}, suffer from the same problem as Chinese and Japanese.
    % \alisa{since multi-word tokens are not popular, I added a bit about why moving to multi-word tokens would be good}
    % Similarly, English tokenizers which feature multi-word tokens have been proposed \citep{gee-etal-2023-multi,kumar-thawani-2022-bpe,liu-etal-2025-superbpe} and suffer from the same problem as Chinese and Japanese.
    \item When completing code, it is common to request completions while in the middle of an identifier \citep{jackson2025character}. 
    % \alisa{really?}
    \item This issue also occurs when performing constrained generation from language models \citep{ribeiro2023guidance}.
    % \alisa{example?}
\end{enumerate} 
In general, the user, unaware of the tokenization, expects samples from the properly conditioned distribution, 
\begin{equation}
    % \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{prompt}} \;\; \triangleq \;\; 
    \Pr{t_1, \ldots, t_n \mid \operatorname{prompt} \sqsubseteq \operatorname{decode}\del{t_1, \ldots, t_n}}\;, \label{eq:cond-true}
\end{equation}
where \(\sqsubseteq\) denotes the prefix relation.
However, the token-prefix conditioned distribution of \cref{eq:cond-naive} and the {\em byte-prefix conditioned distribution}  of \cref{eq:cond-true} can differ substantially (e.g., Figure~\ref{fig:examples}). \cref{eq:cond-true} transcends the arbitrary token boundary set where the user provided prompt stops, decoupling the prompt boundary from token boundaries, to complete the prompt with the {\em exact} distribution from the language model.  
%In these situations, it may be necessary to sample from the proper distribution \cref{eq:cond-true} in order to get good results.
This leads to a fundamental algorithmic question of interest: how do we sample from the byte-prefix conditioned distribution of \cref{eq:cond-true} exactly and efficiently? 


\medskip{\bf Contributions.} 
% We introduce an efficient procedure to convert any BPE tokenizer-based language model into a byte-based language model.
We introduce an efficient procedure to condition a BPE tokenizer-based model on an arbitrary \textit{byte-prefix} given only access to the tokenizer and log-probability queries to the model (\cref{sec:method}).
We demonstrate in experiments that this represents an exact solution to the Prompt Boundary Problem presented above (\cref{sec:language-modeling}).
We show that our method can be used to convert the model into a byte-level language model and that this ability can be used to unify the vocabularies of different models.
% In this work, we present an efficient procedure to compute the next-byte distribution according to 
This enables exact byte-level ensembles of language models with different tokenizers (\cref{sec:ensemble}) and allows one to transfer the post-training of one model onto another model at inference time using proxy-tuning \citep{liu2024tuning} (\cref{sec:proxy-tuning}).
% where the base model and experts have different tokenizers.
We demonstrate in proof-of-concept experiments that language model ensembles and proxy-tuned models constructed with our method are able to outperform their constituent models in downstream evaluations.

