\textbf{Byte Pair Encoding}. BPE was originally presented as a form of data compression in \citet{gage-1994-new} and was proposed for use in NLP in  \citet{sennrich-etal-2016-neural}.
% At a high level, the algorithm forms the final tokens by iteratively merging pairs of tokens.
To tokenize a piece of text with a typical BPE-based tokenizer, the text is first split into chunks, a process called \textit{pretokenization}.
These chunks, or \textit{pretokens}, are then tokenized separately using BPE (thus no token may cross the boundary between pretokens).
The BPE tokenizer processes each pretoken by first converting the text into a sequence of elements of the tokenizer's base vocabulary (common choices for base vocabulary are individual characters or bytes under UTF-8 encoding).
Next, an ordered list of merges is applied to the sequence to form larger tokens.
Each merge specifies a contiguous pair of tokens (which may include products of previous merges), and a new token that represents their concatenation.
The merges are applied left-to-right and once all valid merges are applied, the tokenization is complete.
We show an example application of these steps in \cref{tab:bpe-example}.

