\subsection{Pairwise Validation}\label{sec:pairwise}
Recall that a token sequence is \textit{valid} if it is the encoding of some string under the BPE encoder.\footnote{The notion of valid pairs of tokens was used in \citet{vanantwerpen2024tokens} as the basis for a streaming algorithm and a fast backtracking-based algorithm for BPE tokenization (without addressing the PBP).}
The correctness of the pairwise pruning depends on the following proposition regarding validity under BPE tokenization.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:valid}
Let \(\del{\operatorname{encode}, \operatorname{decode}}\) denote a BPE encoder and decoder pair corresponding to some merge list \(M\) and vocabulary \(V\).
We call a token sequence \(T = \sbr{t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_n} \in V^n\) \textit{valid} if
\(\operatorname{encode}\del{\operatorname{decode}\del{T}} = T\).
Then \(T\) is valid if and only if \(\sbr{t_i, t_{i+1}}\) is valid for all \(i \in \set{0, \ldots, n-1}\). 
\end{proposition}

% In other words, \alisa{this sounds like it's going to be a rephrasing of the proposition, but I think it's a rephrasing of the definition of a valid token seq}
% Since the language model was trained on the output of the BPE encoder, it only ever saw valid sequences during training.
% Thus, valid token sequences represent the exact set of queries the language model is qualified to be evaluated on.
To see that this proposition is true, consider two valid token sequences \(T_1 = \operatorname{encode}\del{S_1}\) and \(T_2 = \operatorname{encode}\del{S_2}\).
If, while tokenizing the concatenation \(S_1 \concat S_2\), 
% \nascomment{never seen that symbol before!  it's not bad.  curious if you came up with that or if it's a convention in other recent work?} \jon{It is a used in some areas of CS (maybe programming languages). The other option I would consider is \(\mathop{\Vert}\). Is that better?}\nascomment{no, I vote to keep as is.  I think maybe I've seen $\oplus$ or $\circ$ but those have other meanings too} 
there is no merge applied that crosses the boundary between \(S_1\) and \(S_2\) then the two strings will ``evolve'' independently, and we will have \(\operatorname{encode}\del{S_1 \concat S_2} = T_1\concat T_2\) which means \(T_1\concat T_2\) is valid.

Conversely, if a merge is applied that does cross the boundary, then the final encoding must feature a token crossing the boundary (since no merge can be undone), which means \(T_1\concat T_2\) cannot be valid since it has no such token.
We depict an example of both cases using OpenAI's cl100k tokenizer \citep{openai2023tokenizer} in \cref{fig:pair}.\footnote{It's worth noting that the analogs of \cref{prop:valid} do \textit{not} hold for either Unigram \citep{kudo2018SentencePiece} or Wordpiece \citep{schuster2012japanese} tokenizers.}

