\subsection{Byte-Level Proxy-Tuning}\label{sec:proxy-tuning}
% \alisa{the first draft is going to be ugly} One popular method of leveraging multiple LLMs for each next-token prediction is via logit arithmetic over each logit distribution, which is then renormalized into a probability distribution \citep{liu-etal-2021-dexperts, li-etal-2023-contrastive, pei-etal-2023-preadd, sennrich-etal-2024-mitigating, Leng_2024_CVPR, shi-etal-2024-trusting, gera-etal-2023-benefits, chuang2024dola, shi-etal-2024-decoding}.
% In this approach, individual LMs act as ``experts'' (if their predictions are combined additively) or ``anti-experts'' (if negatively) \citep{liu-etal-2021-dexperts}.

In addition to additive ensembles over probabilities, the \textit{logit}-level predictions of multiple LMs can be combined via arithmetic, with individual LMs acting as ``experts'' (if their predictions are combined additively) or ``anti-experts'' (if subtractively) \citep{liu-etal-2021-dexperts, li-etal-2023-contrastive, shi-etal-2024-trusting, gera-etal-2023-benefits, chuang2024dola, shi-etal-2024-decoding}.
In particular, this form of ensembling can be used to achieve the \textit{effect} of tuning a large pretrained LM without accessing model weights.
To see how this can be done, note that clearly for logit vectors
\[\vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{tuned}} = \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{base}} + \del{\vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{tuned}} - \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{base}}}.\]
The idea of \textit{proxy-tuning} \cite{liu2024tuning} is to approximate the term \(\vec{\ell}_{\operatorname{tuned}} - \vec{\ell}_{\operatorname{base}}\) using the difference between a pair of tuned and base proxy models \(\vec{\ell}_{\operatorname{expert}} - \vec{\ell}_{\operatorname{anti-expert}}\). 
% , by instead shifting its predictions in the direction of tuning with a small tuned expert and untuned anti-expert (dubbed proxy-tuning; see Figure XXX for an illustration) \cite{liu2024tuning}.
% \[\vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{tuned}} = \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{base}} + \del{\vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{tuned}} - \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{base}}} \approx \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{base}} +  \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{expert}} - \vec{\ell}_{\mathrm{antiexpert}}\]
In our experiments, we proxy-tune a strong base model, \lm{Llama-3.1-8B}, using \lm{Olmo2-1B-Instruct} and \lm{Olmo2-1B} as the expert and anti-expert, respectively, which together represent a strong post-training recipe \cite{olmo-etal-2024-2olmo2furious, lambert-etal-2025-tulu3}.

Shown in \cref{tab:proxy-evals}, we find that the proxy-tuned \lm{Llama 3.1} \citep{llama31} model consistently outperforms the base model alone as well as the small tuned expert.
This highlights a practical application of \methodName{} 
% \nascomment{I think this is the first time the method got a name} 
to ``apply'' post-training to base models without actually training them, thus disentangling the quality of the base model from that of the post-training recipe.

