Given the quark composition of $\Omega_{c}$, its configuration varies depending on the location of the charm quark, so it is divided into $\lambda$-mode and $\rho$-mode when considering the interaction between mesons and quarks,  the amplitude can be written
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{M}&=&\frac{g_{A}^q g_{f}}{2f_{p}}\int d^3\mathbf{\lambda}d^3\mathbf{\rho}e^{-i\mathbf{q_{\lambda}}\cdot\mathbf{\lambda}}e^{-i\mathbf{q_{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\rho}}\\\nonumber
&&\times<Y_{f}|-i[\mathbf{q}\cdot\sigma(1+\frac{\omega_{p}}{2m+M}-\frac{\omega_{p}}{2M})\\\nonumber
&&+\frac{\omega_{p}}{2M}\sigma\cdot(i\mathbf{\nabla_{\lambda}}+2i\mathbf{\nabla_{\rho}})]|Y_{i}>\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
Here, $g_{f}$ is flavor matrix, m and M represent the strange and charm quark masses, respectively. The $q_{\rho}$ $q_{\lambda}$ can be computed as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \mathbf{q_{\lambda}}=\frac{M}{2m+M}\mathbf{q}, 
    \mathbf{q_{\rho}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{q} 
\end{eqnarray}
